# TLS termination in front of the server (task 10.49 / Security Model §4.1,
# §8.6). Three straight gates found no reverse-proxy configuration anywhere
# in the repository — this is that configuration, layered on with
# `docker-compose.tls.yml` rather than baked into the base file, on the same
# reasoning `docker-compose.e2e.yml` and `docker-compose.server-test.yml`
# already use: the production-shaped base file never gains a behavior nobody
# asked for.
#
# TRITRACK_TLS_DOMAIN decides everything about how Caddy gets a certificate,
# and it is required (see docker-compose.tls.yml) rather than defaulted,
# because guessing here is exactly the "shipped default that quietly serves
# cleartext" shape §4.10 exists to prevent:
#
#   - A real, publicly resolvable domain (e.g. tritrack.example.org) — Caddy
#     requests a certificate from Let's Encrypt automatically (ACME, HTTP-01
#     over port 80) and renews it automatically. This is the real-deployment
#     path, and it needs DNS pointed at this host and ports 80/443 reachable
#     from the internet BEFORE it will work — see server/README.md's
#     "TLS termination" section for that human step. It is not something this
#     file, or any automated run, can verify — there is no real host in this
#     repository.
#   - `localhost`, a bare IP, or any other address Caddy's own heuristic
#     recognizes as "not a public name" — Caddy issues from its own internal,
#     locally-trusted CA instead of attempting ACME. This is what makes a
#     local, self-signed verification run possible with the *same* Caddyfile
#     the real deployment uses: nothing here has to change between the two,
#     only what TRITRACK_TLS_DOMAIN is set to.
{$TRITRACK_TLS_DOMAIN} {
	# The origin (task 10.49, alongside this file). `server` already refuses
	# to serve cleartext beyond loopback without either this opt-in or its
	# own TLS configured (Security Model §4.1) — `docker-compose.tls.yml`
	# gives it TRITRACK_TLS_CERT_PATH/TRITRACK_TLS_KEY_PATH pointing at a
	# self-signed pair generated once for exactly this purpose (see
	# server/README.md), so it can run with TRITRACK_ENV=production without
	# tripping over its own cleartext-LAN refusal.
	#
	# `tls_insecure_skip_verify` is deliberate and narrow: it applies only to
	# this one backend hop, over the Docker bridge network — the same trust
	# boundary Postgres already crosses in cleartext (Security Model §2.2) —
	# never to what a client outside this compose network sees, which is the
	# certificate named above, verified normally. The origin's self-signed
	# certificate authenticates nothing to Caddy; its only job is to make
	# `server`'s own "am I speaking TLS" requirement true. Do not widen this
	# to the public-facing side.
	reverse_proxy https://server:8080 {
		transport http {
			tls_insecure_skip_verify
		}

		# Task 10.52 / Security Model §4.4 — finding I-1.
		#
		# **`header_up`, not the default append.** Caddy's reverse_proxy
		# normally *appends* the client address to whatever X-Forwarded-For
		# arrived, so a caller who sends their own header gets it forwarded
		# with the real address tacked on the end. `header_up` with a single
		# value REPLACES the header outright: whatever the caller sent is
		# discarded here, at the first trusted hop, and the origin receives
		# exactly one address that Caddy wrote itself. The origin's parser
		# (`request_identity.dart`) handles a longer chain correctly anyway —
		# but a header with exactly one entry and no attacker contribution has
		# no ambiguity left to get wrong, and this is a boundary worth making
		# unambiguous rather than merely parsed carefully.
		#
		# `{remote_host}` is Caddy's own TCP peer — the client's real address,
		# read from the socket, not from anything the client claimed.
		#
		# **The origin does not trust this header because it is named
		# X-Forwarded-For.** It trusts it only when the TCP peer that sent it
		# is listed in TRITRACK_TRUSTED_PROXIES (set to this container's
		# address in docker-compose.tls.yml). Change one without the other and
		# the origin falls back to counting every request against the proxy's
		# own address — which is finding I-1 exactly, so the two belong
		# together in your head and are cross-referenced in both files.
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
	}
}
